<Guide
  title="Import OneNote Notebooks & Sections | Myelin Notes"
  heading="Move from OneNote to Myelin Notes"
  description="Import a OneNote .onepkg notebook or .one section into Myelin Notes on Mac, Windows, or Linux. Learn the steps and what happens to your text, handwriting, and images."
>
  <h2>Start with an exported notebook or section</h2>
  <p>
    Myelin Notes reads local OneNote files. You do not connect your Microsoft
    account to Myelin or grant it access to your OneDrive. Keep your original
    notebook and export while you check the imported copy.
  </p>
  <p>
    In OneNote for Windows, use File → Export to export a section in OneNote
    format (.one). An existing OneNote package (.onepkg) is also supported by
    Myelin. Export options vary between OneNote versions and accounts; see
    <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onenote/onenote-platform-data-protection-recovery">Microsoft’s platform and export reference</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For notebooks on personal OneDrive, Microsoft also provides a
    <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onenote/export-and-import-onenote-notebooks">notebook export through OneNote for the web</a>.
    Extract the downloaded archive and import its .one section files individually.
    Myelin’s OneNote importer accepts .one and .onepkg files, not the ZIP archive.
    Microsoft’s web notebook export does not support work or school OneDrive
    accounts or SharePoint notebooks.
  </p>

  <h2>Import the file into Myelin Notes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open Myelin Notes and choose Import from the library’s create menu.</li>
    <li>Select Import from OneNote, then choose your .onepkg or .one file.</li>
    <li>Review the page and section counts shown after the file is read.</li>
    <li>If the destination folder name already exists, choose Keep both (rename) to retain the existing folder.</li>
    <li>Start the import, then review the completion summary for any pages that could not be imported.</li>
    <li>Open a few imported notes and compare their text, diagrams, and images with the originals.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    The import creates a root folder named after your file. Sections and section
    groups become folders, and imported pages become canvas notes. Importing is a
    one-time copy: later changes in OneNote do not update the Myelin notes.
  </p>

  <h2>What transfers, and what changes?</h2>
  <table>
    <caption class="text-left font-medium">OneNote import support in Myelin Notes</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Content</th><th scope="col">Imported result</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Text</th><td>Editable text boxes. Text outlines use a single style, so mixed formatting is simplified.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Handwriting</th><td>Ink strokes retain their paths and colors. Per-point pen pressure is not preserved.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Images</th><td>Images become canvas elements. Images that cannot be decoded are skipped.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Tables</th><td>Text rows with tab-separated cells, rather than editable table cells.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Attachments</th><td>File attachments are not imported. Keep or export those files separately.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Start with one representative section, especially if your notebooks contain
    complex formatting or attachments. A successful page import does not mean
    every kind of content on that page was preserved.
  </p>

  <h2>If you can only export a PDF</h2>
  <p>
    You can also <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-US/OneNote/onenote-help-and-learning/export-notes-from-onenote-as-a-pdf">export notes from OneNote as a PDF</a>
    and bring that PDF into Myelin through the Files importer. This keeps a static
    document you can annotate. Its existing text and handwriting do not become
    individually editable Myelin elements.
  </p>
  <p>
    After importing, you can add new handwriting and typed notes around your
    material. Read about <a href="/guides/linux-handwriting-notes/">handwriting and PDF notes on Linux</a>
    or <a href="/guides/offline-notes/">working offline on Mac, Windows, and Linux</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
